  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Sunday) \nIssued at 140 AM MDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nThere will be a downtick in the coverage of showers and\nthunderstorms on Thursday as the center of high pressure aloft migrates\nwestward near the NM/TX border. There will probably then be an \nuptick in storm coverage from the northern mountains eastward on\nFriday as a disturbance clipping northeast NM in northwest flow\naloft sends a moist backdoor front into the state and nudges the\nupper high pressure center southwestward over southern NM.\nNorthwest flow aloft is notorious for producing strong to severe\nstorms over northeast and east central NM, and there will probably\nbe at least a few of these on Friday afternoon and evening. The\nweekend ahead should continue to be convectively active thanks to\nmoisture introduced by Friday's backdoor front. In addition, \nthere are signs in some models that the disturbance that clips \nnortheast NM on Friday will at least partially drift eastward over\nNM Saturday and Sunday as the upper high pressure center drifts \nto the north and west of the state. \n\nWith the upper high gradually moving overhead, high temperatures \nwill trend warmer during the latter half of the week and the\nweekend. High temperatures will reach the low 100s across the \neast central and southeast plains each afternoon starting\nWednesday and more-so Thursday, and around 100 in the central \nvalley starting Friday, creating moderate to major heat risk at\nlower elevations from the central valley eastward.",
